b9b7b125b99f4e259bde169ea1426bc73951562843fcabb437b348f8e72" "basis" 1928 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A rectangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "19e86b9b7b125b99f4e259bde169ea1426bc73951562843fcabb437b348f8e72" "basis" 1928 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "A triangle"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "19e86b9b7b125b99f4e259bde169ea1426bc73951562843fcabb437b348f8e72" "basis" 1928 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/state.json" "file_sha256" "19e86b9b7b125b99f4e259bde169ea1426bc73951562843fcabb437b348f8e72" "basis" 1928 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14060375/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "827e2d0c67871315f13e9696a969ecd768ed6615275885cde1daeb05656b49d3" "basis" 1435 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/state.json" "file_sha256" "827e2d0c67871315f13e9696a969ecd768ed6615275885cde1daeb05656b49d3" "basis" 1435 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21065" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_captur