on-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-147605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 10 & 0 & -20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,10)$, $(0,0),$ $(4,-20).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/8c86cb108725b3f1c7b5731dda0f11a5cb21a7daa023c5153cce1ece3ba6ba0f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-147605" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "014145b536e805c623c21e221df009f1f835d6644d02617fa95369f2b0f55c04"} {"question" "q-147605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-147605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/5e316ceff92e78155ee352a2e5c3c526d30852fb1803fd9cfb938bea3e5cf984.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/6ba878568654dc85dba3ac561617132f851e62b63473eb8239fa716f53cd0dcd.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/d3f815e4205f0a24a125162072843fe507d0683ce40a89fdd13ee29c296f7500.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/dfa05a8c2c943d5f74e5e5fef468006117c97b2d3d0a7b7a6b9c2024b6089272.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-147605" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/b6fe02d5b3d96aeaa6a4edc9a027ec69993330252322e10a57618dd499bb02b5.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/state.json" "file_sha256" "42e2b57ed960de2618cf5cff5767e8325dde98f8c1de76f863d5d0f039cf982e"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14073713/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "014145b536e805c623c21e221df009f1f835d6644d02617fa95369f2b0f55c04"} {"question" "q-162150" "field" "selection" "at